        "content": "Understanding the philosophical, economic, and technical boundaries separating Natural Farming from Organic and Conventional systems is vital for agricultural professionals.\n\n| Feature | Conventional Chemical Farming | Certified Organic Farming | Natural Farming (ZBNF / BPKP) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Philosophical Premise** | Anthropocentric input-output optimization; chemical substitution | Environmental substitution of synthetic chemicals with organic bio-inputs | Nature-mimicking; zero external inputs; self-regenerative |\n| **Input Sourcing** | Industrial factory-synthesized chemicals (Urea, DAP, Pyrethroids) | Commercially purchased off-farm bio-inputs (Vermicompost, Bio-NPK, Neem oil) | 100% prepared on-farm using native cow dung/urine, local plants, and virgin soil |\n| **Tillage & Plowing** | Deep, intensive mechanical tillage and soil inversion | Conventional mechanical tillage and harrowing | Zero to minimum shallow surface tillage; no plowing |\n| **Compost / FYM Use** | Chemical focus; limited FYM (5\u201310 t/ha) | Massive application of vermicompost / FYM (10\u201320 t/ha; bulky) | No heavy compost/vermicompost; relies on small microbial inoculants (Jeevamrit) |\n| **Production Costs** | Extremely high; volatile cash expenses for agrochemicals | High to very high due to expensive certified organic inputs | Near zero; eliminates all purchased off-farm commercial inputs |\n| **Certification Model** | Not applicable; residue testing compliance | Third-party commercial certification (NPOP; expensive, bureaucratic) | Community Participatory Guarantee System (PGS-India; farmer-led, low cost) |\n| **Environmental Impact** | High pollution, groundwater depletion, pesticide residues | Reduced chemical pollution, but high carbon footprint in hauling bulky compost | Maximum carbon sequestration, biodiversity restoration, 50-70% water savings |",
