      "difficulty": "Easy",
      "syllabusTopic": "Subhash Palekar's Zero Budget Natural Farming (ZBNF) model: origins, principles, and transition to BPKP",
      "questionText": "Describe the genesis of Subhash Palekar's Zero Budget Natural Farming (ZBNF) and explain why it is termed 'Zero Budget'.",
      "modelAnswer": "Subhash Palekar, an agricultural graduate from Vidarbha, Maharashtra, developed Zero Budget Natural Farming (ZBNF) during the late 1990s. Palekar initially practiced Green Revolution chemical agriculture on his family farm, but after a decade observed declining crop responses, hardened soils, and skyrocketing fertilizer requirements. Simultaneously, the Vidarbha cotton belt witnessed an unprecedented agrarian crisis characterized by farmer indebtedness and suicides driven by high expenditures on hybrid seeds, synthetic chemicals, and tubewell electricity.\n\n### The 'Zero Budget' Concept\n- **Zero External Cash Outflow:** In agricultural accounting, 'budget' refers to the cash expenses incurred on purchased inputs. 'Zero Budget' means the farmer incurs near-zero production expenses because all inputs\u2014seed treatments, bio-inoculants, pest repellents, and soil amendments\u2014are prepared entirely on-farm using native materials (cow dung, urine, jaggery, flour, local flora).\n- **Bonus Intercrop Revenue:** ZBNF mandates intercropping and multi-tier polyculture. The revenue generated from short-duration companion crops (e.g., pulses, oilseeds, vegetables) fully recovers the minimal operational costs of the main cash crop, rendering the net cost of the primary crop effectively zero.\n- **Policy Evolution:** The model has since been institutionalized by the Government of India as **Bharatiya Prakritik Krishi Paddhati (BPKP)**.",
