      "modelAnswer": "Although Natural Farming and Certified Organic Farming both reject synthetic chemical pesticides and fertilizers, they differ fundamentally in operational philosophy and economic architecture:\n\n1. **Input Sourcing and Commercial Dependency:**\n   - *Certified Organic Farming:* Permits and often relies heavily on commercially purchased off-farm organic inputs, such as factory-produced vermicompost, bone meal, rock phosphate, neem oil formulations, and certified bio-fertilizer packets.\n   - *Natural Farming:* Strictly prohibits purchased off-farm inputs. Everything is prepared 100% on-farm using native biological resources: dung and urine from an indigenous (*Bos indicus*) cow, local botanicals, jaggery, pulse flour, and virgin field soil.\n2. **Cost of Cultivation:**\n   - *Certified Organic Farming:* Often entails high production costs due to the purchase of expensive commercial organic bio-inputs and the labor needed to haul massive volumes of manure (10\u201320 t/ha).\n   - *Natural Farming:* Operates on a 'zero-budget' principle with near-zero cash outlays, as all formulations are prepared by the farm family at negligible cost.\n3. **Certification System:**\n   - *Certified Organic Farming:* Requires formal third-party certification (e.g., NPOP / ISO 17065) conducted by commercial auditing companies, which is expensive, bureaucratic, and inaccessible to smallholders.\n   - *Natural Farming:* Relies on the **Participatory Guarantee System (PGS-India)**\u2014a low-cost, decentralized peer-review certification operated directly by local farmer groups.",
