      "questionText": "What is the Bharatiya Prakritik Krishi Paddhati (BPKP) and how does it institutionalize Natural Farming in India?",
      "modelAnswer": "The **Bharatiya Prakritik Krishi Paddhati (BPKP)** is a dedicated sub-scheme launched by the Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare, Government of India, under the centrally sponsored Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana (PKVY) to formally promote and scale Natural Farming across the nation.\n\n### Key Objectives and Framework of BPKP\n1. **Promotion of Traditional On-Farm Farming:** BPKP aims to phase out synthetic chemical fertilizers and pesticides, replacing them with farm-derived biological formulations rooted in indigenous cow ecology and traditional agricultural wisdom.\n2. **Financial and Operational Support:** The scheme provides financial assistance of **Rs. 12,200 per hectare for three years** to cluster-based farming groups. This funding covers farmer capacity building, hands-on field demonstrations, exposure visits, and the establishment of on-farm input preparation infrastructure.\n3. **Continuous Extension via 'Community Resource Persons' (CRPs):** BPKP utilizes practicing natural farmers as champion Community Resource Persons to train peer farmers through farmer-to-farmer knowledge exchange.\n4. **Certification Integration:** Mandates the adoption of the decentralized **Participatory Guarantee System (PGS-India)** to certify natural farming clusters without imposing commercial auditing fees, facilitating market access for residue-free produce.",
