      "id": "AGR217-U1-SUB15",
      "questionType": "COMPARISON",
      "maxMarks": 5,
      "difficulty": "Medium",
      "syllabusTopic": "Comparative paradigm: Natural Farming vs. Organic Farming vs. Conventional Chemical Farming",
      "questionText": "Compare Natural Farming with Certified Organic Farming across input source, weed management, composting approach, and certification cost.",
      "modelAnswer": "Natural Farming and Certified Organic Farming diverge significantly across their technical, economic, and operational frameworks:\n\n1. **Input Sourcing and Origin:** Certified Organic Farming permits the purchase of commercial off-farm biological products, such as packaged bio-fertilizers, commercial vermicompost, bone meal, rock phosphate, and branded bio-pesticides. Natural Farming strictly prohibits purchasing commercial inputs, requiring all preparations (Jeevamrit, Beejamrit, botanical sprays) to be prepared 100% on-farm using native cow (*Bos indicus*) dung/urine and local flora.\n2. **Weed Management:** Certified Organic Farming frequently uses mechanical tractor tillage, power weeders, and manual hoeing to control weeds. Natural Farming strictly avoids mechanical weed cultivation, suppressing weeds biologically through continuous surface straw mulching (*Shushka Acchadana*) and living cover crops (*Jeeva Acchadana*).\n3. **Composting and Manure Philosophy:** Organic farming applies heavy doses of bulky organic manures (10\u201320 t/ha of vermicompost or composted FYM). Natural Farming rejects large-scale composting and vermicomposting, applying low volumes of concentrated fermented microbial cultures (Jeevamrit @ 200 L/acre) that activate native soil biology.\n4. **Certification Model and Economics:** Organic farming relies on formal third-party certification (e.g., NPOP / USDA Organic) involving costly annual inspection fees. Natural Farming utilizes the **Participatory Guarantee System (PGS-India)**\u2014a low-cost, decentralized peer-review framework managed by local farmer groups.",
      "outlinePoints": [
        "Input origin: purchased off-farm bio-inputs (organic) vs 100% on-farm cow/botanical preparations (natural)",
        "Weed control: mechanical tillage/hoeing vs biological straw and live legume mulching",
        "Manure management: heavy 10-20 t/ha bulky compost vs 200 L fermented Jeevamrit microbial bio-cultures",
        "Certification: expensive third-party commercial auditing (NPOP) vs community peer-review (PGS-India)"
      ]
