      "syllabusTopic": "Concept, philosophy, and history of Natural Farming; Masanobu Fukuoka's One-Straw Revolution",
      "questionText": "Compare the philosophy and technical methodologies of Masanobu Fukuoka (One-Straw Revolution) with Subhash Palekar (Zero Budget Natural Farming).",
      "modelAnswer": "Masanobu Fukuoka and Subhash Palekar are the two foremost pioneers of modern Natural Farming, sharing core ecological principles while adapting them to distinct geographic and cultural contexts:\n\n1. **Philosophical Orientation:**\n   - *Fukuoka:* Deeply influenced by Zen Buddhism, Taoism, and the concept of *Mu* (non-attachment/nothingness). He viewed farming as a spiritual path toward human character perfection and non-discriminating harmony with nature.\n   - *Palekar:* Formulated ZBNF as a practical socio-economic solution to smallholder farmer indebtedness, rural poverty, and agrarian suicides in semi-arid tropical India.\n2. **Role of Livestock and Animals:**\n   - *Fukuoka:* Minimal reliance on domestic livestock. He did not incorporate cow dung or animal urine into his cropping cycles, relying entirely on wild birds, unchopped plant straw, and white clover cover crops.\n   - *Palekar:* The indigenous Indian cow (*Bos indicus*) is the indispensable central engine of ZBNF. Fresh cow dung and urine form the mandatory biological backbone for Beejamrit, Jeevamrit, and pest repellents.\n3. **Sowing Technology:**\n   - *Fukuoka:* Developed clay seed balls (*Tsuchi Dango*) to broadcast mixed seeds onto untilled, weed-covered fields, allowing seasonal rain to dissolve the clay for germination.\n   - *Palekar:* Relies on biological seed coating (*Beejamrit*) followed by conventional furrow seeding or dibbling into ridges and furrows.\n4. **Irrigation Strategy:**\n   - *Fukuoka:* Avoided flooded rice paddies, growing rice under rainfed upland conditions alongside seasonal rainfall in humid temperate Japan.\n   - *Palekar:* Developed the **Waaphasa** irrigation doctrine for semi-arid tropics, utilizing alternate-furrow watering and mulching to maximize aeration and save 50\u201370% water.",
      "outlinePoints": [
        "Philosophical roots: Zen spiritual non-action (Fukuoka) vs socio-economic debt elimination (Palekar)",
        "Role of livestock: zero animal reliance (Fukuoka) vs Bos indicus cow as indispensable biological engine (Palekar)",
        "Sowing methodology: clay seed balls (Tsuchi Dango) vs Beejamrit microbial seed coating and furrow placement",
        "Water management: temperate rainfed upland systems vs tropical Waaphasa alternate-furrow irrigation",
        "Shared core: absolute rejection of synthetic agrochemicals and mechanical soil plowing"
      ]
